The meaning of any word or phrase not otherwise listed below shall be ascribed its regular and ordinary meaning.
City means the municipal corporation known as the City of Fairburn, Georgia, including its departments, officers, boards, commissions, agencies, and legally constituted authorities and subdivisions.
City attorney means the duly appointed legal counsel for the City of Fairburn, whether serving under contract or employment, including all assistant or deputy city attorneys acting within the scope of their assigned authority.
City Charter means the Charter of the City of Fairburn, Georgia, as adopted and amended by the General Assembly or applicable federal law or entity, defines the roles, powers, and limitations of elected and appointed officials, as well as the city's governing structure.
Covered claim means subject to the conditions and exclusions provided herein or as otherwise precluded by law, any legal action, complaint, investigation, or proceeding—civil, administrative, or quasi-criminal—-brought against a covered individual that arises out of or relates directly to the performance of that individual's official duties and for which approval has been granted by [the] mayor and council as provided herein. Covered claim may include:
(1) Civil litigation (e.g., tort claims, Section 1983 claims, zoning disputes);
(2) Ethics complaints;
(3) Administrative charges (e.g., EEOC, GBI, or other agency filings);
(4) Quasi-criminal citations or claims that stem directly from authorized municipal action.
Covered individual means any eligible person who was serving the city at the time of the act or omission giving rise to the covered claim. "Covered individual" shall mean:
(1) Elected officials. The mayor and members of the city council.
(2) Appointed officials, including, but not limited to:
a. City administrator and assistant city administrator;
b. City clerk and deputy clerks;
c. City attorney, deputy and assistant city attorneys;
d. Municipal court judges, solicitor, and public defender;
e. Members of city boards, commissions, authorities, or committees as duly appointed by mayor or council (e.g., ethics board, planning commission).
(3) City employee. Full-time, part-time, seasonal, temporary, and contract personnel employed by the city.
(4) Volunteers. Individuals formally authorized to act on behalf of the city and where mayor and council has specially designated such individual to be covered hereunder and where the act giving rise to the claim occurred during such authorization. Coverage shall extend to qualifying acts performed during service, regardless of whether the claim is made during or after the individual's period of service.
Eligible or eligibility means a covered individual who has timely and properly complied with the provisions of this division, has been approved by mayor and council as provided herein, and has not been determined to have engaged in an excluded or non-covered act.
Excluded acts or non-covered acts means any act, omission, or course of conduct that disqualifies a covered individual from receiving legal defense or indemnification under this division. Excluded acts include, but are not limited to, circumstances known or subsequently discovered in which:
(1) The act or omission did not arise out of and in the course of his/her employment or is unconnected to the official's public duties:
(2) The employee or official acted or failed to act because of actual fraud, corruption, or actual malice on his/her part;
(3) Provision of a defense against or indemnification of the claim or proceeding would not be in the best interests of the city;
(4) Limitation of a defense against or indemnification of the claim or proceeding would be in the best interests of the city;
(5) The proceeding is a criminal prosecution unrelated to or otherwise not emanating from or connected to any underlying civil proceeding; provided, however, that a defense shall not be refused where the criminal prosecution is one for an alleged violation of the Georgia Open Records Act or Georgia Open Meetings Act and it is determined that the charged employee or public official has acted in good faith to comply with the subject Act and has substantially complied with all city policies relating to the subject Act, which determination shall be made by the council;
(6) The request was not timely or properly made;
(7) The claim arises out of an act or omission at a time when the employee or official was under the influence of drugs or intoxicants;
(8) The act or omission giving rise to the claims was in direct disobedience of an order or instructions of a superior or in contravention of established city policy;
(9) The employee or official fails to cooperate in the defense of a claim or proceeding that arises after the effective date of the ordinance from which this division is derived;
(10) Conduct undertaken outside the scope of lawful authority;
(11) Intentional violations of federal or state law, city Charter, city ordinances, or the city ethics code;
(12) Violations of state or federal employment laws (e.g., Title VII, ADA, FMLA, FLSA);
(13) Misuse of public funds, theft, or criminal misconduct;
(14) Acts taken in bad faith, with malice, or for personal gain;
(15) Failure to timely notify the city attorney of a request under this division or refusal to cooperate with administrative processes related to this division or fail or refuse to assist in the city's defense or mitigation efforts;
(16) The act is otherwise fully covered by the city's insurer. There shall be no double-dipping or stacking coverage for amounts within the limits of the insured claim;
(17) A conflict of interest is created by virtue of the employment of a defense strategy that involves blaming the city or claiming that the city's policies or lack of training caused harm;
(18) The city itself brings a lawsuit as either initial claim and/or a counterclaim against an employee, elected or appointed official, as a result of an internal investigation, or violations of city ordinances, the city Charter, city resolutions, the city's personnel policy and procedures manual, procedural rules and breaches of conduct.
Good faith means actions undertaken with an honest belief that the conduct is lawful, ethical, and authorized by the individual's role, without intent to deceive, retaliate, harass, or violate applicable law or policy. Good faith excludes reckless, intentional or knowingly unlawful conduct.
Indemnification means the city's payment or reimbursement of legal expenses, judgments, settlements, or other costs incurred by a covered individual in connection with a covered claim, subject to the covered individual's eligibility and subsequent approval by mayor and council as provided in this division. Indemnification may be full or partial and may be conditioned upon reimbursement rights. Indemnification may be revoked by [the] mayor and council as provided herein.
Legal defense means the provision or funding of legal representalion and related litigation services for a covered individual in connection with a covered claim for an approved amount as provided herein. This includes attorney fees, court filings, expert costs, discovery, depositions, and motion practice. Appellate work is not covered and shall be subject to the same notice and approval process as the initial coverage request.
Misrepresentation or omission means any false, misleading, incomplete, or deceptive statement, whether made knowingly or recklessly, in connection with a request for legal defense or indemnification leading to a determination that would disqualify coverage. Examples include, but are not limited to:
(1) Omitting relevant facts that would affect eligibility or status as a covered individual;
(2) Mischaracterizing the nature or scope of the underlying conduct for which coverage is requested;
(3) Withholding relevant communications or documentation;
(4) Any misrepresentation or omission—whether during the application or while covered—may serve as an independent basis for denial or revocation of coverage.
No sum means the non-payment or excluded payment of any amount whatsoever including, but not limited to, compensatory damages, attorney's fees and costs.
Notice means a written request submitted to the city attorney by a covered individual seeking defense or indemnification as prescribed in this division. Failure to submit a timely or complete notice as required in this division may result in the denial of eligibility to qualify as a covered individual.
Official duties means responsibilities, actions, or decisions performed within the scope of lawful authority assigned by the city Charter, ordinances, administrative policy, or formal delegation. Examples include the lawful act of:
(1) Voting or participating in meetings;
(2) Issuing permits or regulatory decisions;
(3) Providing legal advice, policy enforcement, or staff supervision (when authorized);
(4) Making official communications on behalf of the city as authorized or directed by the proper authority.
Proceeding means any matter of a civil nature in or before any court or agency of this state or of any other state or of the United States.
Reimbursement means the obligation of a covered individual to repay the city for legal defense costs, indemnity payments, or litigation-related expenses previously paid by the city, if:
(1) Coverage is later denied or revoked; or
(2) The individual provided false, fraudulent or misleading information in his/her effort to either be eligible or approved for indemnification under this division; or
(3) The individual is found to have committed an excluded act that renders him/her ineligible for indemnification under this division; or
(4) Reimbursement is required by law, order, or contract.
Revocation means the city's formal withdrawal of defense or indemnification rights previously granted under this division due to:
(1) Discovery of facts that would have rendered the individual ineligible at the time coverage was granted; or
(2) Evidence of misrepresentation or omission; or
(3) Revocation may be applied retroactively and includes the right of the city to demand and receive full reimbursement of previously paid expenses. [The] mayor and council shall determine revocation by way of majority vote following consideration of the city attorney's written recommendation. Such vote shall authorize the city attorney to initiate and pursue legal efforts to recover the amount demanded to the fullest extent of the law.
Ultra vires acts (excluded acts) means any intentional or knowing act committed outside the lawful authority of a covered individual, whether or not specifically enumerated. Ultra vires conduct includes, but is not limited to:
(1) Issuing directives to staff in violation of the city Charter; ordinance; personnel policy, resolutions and/or other City of Fairburn governing documents;
(2) Interfering in personnel decisions outside one's role;
(3) Representing a city policy or action without authorization;
(4) Retaliation, harassment, or self-dealing under the pretense of official capacity;
(5) Any act excluded from indemnity or defense as provided herein;
(6) This definition is illustrative, not exhaustive and includes any act that exceeds the scope of lawful public service as reasonably defined by state or federal law, city Charter, ordinances, or official delegation.
