270:
(a) The following words, terms and phrases, when used in this section, shall have the meanings ascribed to them in this subsection, except where the context clearly indicates a different meaning:
Dangerous dog.
(1) The term "dangerous dog" means:
a. Any dog that inflicts a severe injury on a human being without provocation on public or private property at any time; or
b. Any dog that aggressively bites, attacks or endangers the safety of humans without provocation after the dog has been classified as a potentially dangerous dog and after the owner has been notified of such classification.
(2) A dog is not considered "dangerous" if it bites a human:
a. When being used by a law enforcement officer;
b. When its owner is being attacked;
c. Who is a willful trespasser on the property of the owner or who is committing another tort or crime;
d. Who has tormented or abused it or who in the past has been observed or reported to have tormented or abused it.
Dog control officer means the director of code enforcement or his designee.
Owner means any natural person or any legal entity, including, but not limited to, a corporation, partnership, firm or trust owning, possessing, harboring, keeping or having custody or control of a dangerous dog, potentially dangerous dog or vicious dog within the city.
Potentially dangerous dog means any dog that, without provocation, bites a human being on public or private property at any time. A dog is not considered "potentially dangerous" if it bites a human:
(1) When being used by a law enforcement officer;
(2) When its owner is being attacked;
(3) Who is a willful trespasser on the property of the owner or who is committing another tort or crime;
(4) Who has tormented or abused it or who in the past has been observed or reported to have tormented or abused it.
Proper enclosure means an enclosure for keeping a dangerous dog, potentially dangerous dog or vicious dog while on the owner's property securely confined indoors or in a securely enclosed and locked pen, fence or structure suitable to prevent the entry of young children and designed to prevent the dog from escaping. Any such pen or structure shall have secure sides and a secure top, and, if the dog is enclosed within a fence, all sides of the fence shall be of sufficient height and the bottom of the fence shall be constructed or secured in such a manner as to prevent the dog's escape either from over or from under the fence. Any such enclosure shall also provide protection from the elements for the dog.
Records of an appropriate authority means records of any state, county or municipal law enforcement agency; records of any county or municipal animal control agency; records of any county board of health, records of any federal, state or local court; or records of a dog control officer provided for in this article.
Severe injury means any physical injury that results in broken bones or disfiguring lacerations requiring multiple sutures or cosmetic surgery or a physical injury that results in death.
Vicious dog means any animal which, because of temperament, conditioning or training, has a known propensity, tendency, or disposition to attack, bite or injure humans or other animals without provocation; or an animal which has on one or more occasions caused physical injury to humans or other animals without provocation; whether on public or private property. A dog is not considered vicious if it attacks, bites or menaces:
(1) When being used by a law enforcement officer;
(2) Anyone attacking the owner;
(3) Unlawful trespassers on the property of the owner;
(4) Any person or animal that has tormented or abused it;
(5) If it is defending its young or other animal.
(b) A dog that inflicts an injury upon a person when the dog is being used by a law enforcement officer to carry out the law enforcement officer's official duties shall not be a dangerous dog, potentially dangerous dog or vicious dog within the meaning of this article. A dog shall not be a dangerous dog, a potentially dangerous dog or vicious dog within the meaning of this article if the injury inflicted by the dog was sustained by a person who, at the time, was committing a willful trespass or other tort or was tormenting, abusing or assaulting the dog or had in the past been observed or reported to have tormented, abused or assaulted the dog or was committing or attempting to commit a crime.
(Code 1966, § 4-50; Ord. No. 93-19, § 2, 10-25-1993; Ord. No. 94-01, § 1, 1-10-1994)

273:
(a) As applied to the owners of potentially dangerous dogs or vicious dogs, the procedures provided for in this section must be carried out as a necessary condition for the enforcement of the provisions of this article against such owners. As applied to the owners of dangerous dogs, the procedures provided for in this section shall not be an essential element of any crime provided for in this article.
(b) When a dangerous dog, potentially dangerous dog or vicious dog is classified as such, the dog control officer shall notify the dog's owner of such classification.
(c) The notice to the owner shall meet the following requirements:
(1) The notice shall be in writing and mailed by certified mail to the owner's last known address;
(2) The notice shall include a summary of the dog control officer's findings that formed the basis for the dog's classification as a dangerous dog, potentially dangerous dog or vicious dog;
(3) The notice shall be dated and shall state that the owner, within 15 days after the date shown on the notice, has a right to request a hearing on the dog control officer's determination that the dog is a dangerous dog, a potentially dangerous dog or a vicious dog;
(4) The notice shall state that the hearing, if requested, shall be before the city animal control board;
(5) The notice shall state that if a hearing is not requested, the dog control officer's determination that the dog is a dangerous dog, a potentially dangerous dog or a vicious dog will become effective for all purposes under this article on a date specified in the notice, which shall be after the last day on which the owner has a right to request a hearing; and
(6) The notice shall include a form to request a hearing before the animal control board and shall provide specific instructions on mailing or delivery of such request to the animal control board.
(d) When the animal control board receives a request for a hearing as provided in subsection (c) of this section, it shall schedule such hearing within 30 days after receiving the request. The animal control board shall notify the dog owner in writing by certified mail of the date, time and place of the hearing, and such notice shall be mailed to the dog owner at least ten days prior to the date of the hearing. At the hearing, the owner of the dog shall be given the opportunity to testify and present evidence and, in addition thereto, the animal control board shall receive such other evidence and hear such other testimony as the animal control board may find reasonably necessary to make a determination either to sustain, modify or overrule the dog control officer's classification of the dog.
(e) Within ten days of the date of the hearing, the animal control board shall notify the dog owner in writing by certified mail of its determination on the matter. If such determination is that the dog is a dangerous dog, a potentially dangerous dog or vicious dog, the notice shall specify the date upon which that determination is effective.
(Code 1966, § 4-52; Ord. No. 93-19, § 2, 10-25-1993; Ord. No. 94-01, § 1, 1-10-1994)

277:
The owner of a dangerous dog, potentially dangerous dog or vicious dog shall be solely liable for any injury to or death of a person caused by such dog. Under no circumstances shall the city or any employee or official of the city that enforces or fails to enforce the provisions of this article be held liable for any damages to any person who suffers an injury inflicted:
(1) By a dog that has been identified as being a dangerous dog, potentially dangerous dog or vicious dog;
(2) By a dog which the city has failed to identify as a dangerous dog, potentially dangerous dog or vicious dog;
(3) By a dog which has been identified as being a dangerous dog, potentially dangerous dog or vicious dog but has not been kept or restrained in the manner described in this article; or
(4) By a dangerous dog, potentially dangerous dog or vicious dog whose owner has not maintained insurance coverage or a surety bond as required in this article.
(Code 1966, § 4-57; Ord. No. 93-19, § 2, 10-25-1993; Ord. No. 94-01, § 1, 1-10-1994)
